    </div>

    <!-- Subtle ambient lighting -->
    <div class="pointer-events-none absolute top-1/4 right-0 w-[500px] h-[500px] bg-gold/8 blur-3xl rounded-full" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="pointer-events-none absolute bottom-1/4 left-0 w-[500px] h-[500px] bg-accent-tertiary/6 blur-3xl rounded-full" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 relative z-10 space-y-12 sm:space-y-14">
      
      <!-- Section Orientation Heading -->
      <div class="text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-3">
        <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-widest block">
          Hermeneutical Comparative Matrix · ལྟ་གྲུབ་ཤན་འབྱེད།
        </span>
        <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display text-ink font-normal leading-tight m-0">
          The Two Truths: Rangtong & Shentong in Juxtaposition
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-muted font-serif leading-relaxed m-0 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
          How the two major Madhyamaka hermeneutical traditions analyze conventional appearances, the status of ultimate reality, and the nature of Buddha-nature.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Main Full-Width 2-Column Split -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8 lg:gap-10 items-stretch">
        
        <!-- Left Column: Rangtong (Conventional Truth / Self-Emptiness) -->
        <div class="surface-card rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8 lg:p-10 border-l-4 border-l-accent border border-border shadow-card flex flex-col justify-between relative overflow-hidden group bg-surface">
          <!-- Asymmetric Negation Glyph Watermark -->
          <div class="pointer-events-none absolute -bottom-6 -right-4 text-accent/8 font-mono text-8xl select-none font-bold" aria-hidden="true">∅</div>

          <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 relative z-10 flex-grow mb-6 sm:mb-8 lg:mb-10">
            
            <!-- Column Header -->
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-b border-border pb-5">
              <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
                <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-widest leading-[1.6]">
                  Conventional Truth · ཀུན་རྫོབ་བདེན་པ།
                </span>
                <span class="text-sm sm:text-base font-tibetan text-accent font-bold leading-[1.6]">རང་སྟོང་།</span>
              </div>
              <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-display font-bold text-ink leading-tight m-0">
                Rangtong: "Empty of Self"
              </h3>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono text-muted m-0">
                Prāsaṅgika Madhyamaka · Gelug & Scholastic Sakya (Nāgārjuna & Candrakīrti)
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Central Metaphor Highlight -->
            <div class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-subtle border border-border flex flex-col gap-3">
              <h4 class="font-display text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">
                The Unhewn Block of Marble
              </h4>
              <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
                Buddha-nature is <strong>raw material with workable potential</strong>, but has no finished features yet. Like a block of marble, it requires years of sustained effort by a sculptor (accumulating merit and wisdom over eons) to produce a statue that did not previously exist. The enlightened qualities of a Buddha are <em>newly produced</em> by the path.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Deep Doctrinal Explanation -->
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-sm sm:text-base text-ink/90 font-serif leading-relaxed">
              <p class="m-0">
                The Rangtong position holds that <strong>all phenomena without exception — including ultimate truth itself — are empty of self-nature (<em>svabhāva</em>)</strong>. Emptiness is defined as a <em>non-affirming negative</em> (<em>prasajya-pratiṣedha</em>): the sheer absence of inherent existence, full stop. It does not point to any hidden positive ground behind the negation.
              </p>
              <p class="m-0">
                On this view, when the Buddha spoke of "Buddha-nature" in the Third Turning sūtras, he was using <strong>provisional, pedagogical language (<em>neyārtha</em>)</strong> designed to encourage sentient beings — not describing an actually existent luminous entity. Buddha-nature is simply the <em>mere emptiness of the mind</em>, its natural openness to transformation.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>

          <!-- Bottom Denial Box -->
          <div class="border-t border-border flex flex-col gap-2 bg-surface-subtle/70 -mx-6 sm:-mx-8 lg:-mx-10 -mb-6 sm:-mb-8 lg:-mb-10 p-6 sm:p-8 lg:p-10 rounded-b-3xl relative z-10">
            <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-wider block">
              What Rangtong Denies:
            </span>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
              That ultimate reality possesses any positive, inherent qualities of its own. Everything — including the deepest truth — is characterized by the bare absence of self-nature. There is nothing "behind" or "beyond" emptiness.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Right Column: Shentong (Ultimate Truth / Other-Emptiness) -->
        <div class="surface-card rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8 lg:p-10 border-l-4 border-l-gold border border-gold/40 shadow-card ring-1 ring-gold/20 flex flex-col justify-between relative overflow-hidden group bg-gradient-to-br from-surface via-surface to-gold/5">
