          <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 relative z-10 flex-grow mb-6 sm:mb-8 lg:mb-10">
            
            <!-- Column Header -->
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 border-b border-border pb-5">
              <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline justify-between gap-2">
                <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent-secondary uppercase tracking-widest leading-[1.6]">
                  Ultimate Truth · དོན་དམ་བདེན་པ།
                </span>
                <span class="text-sm sm:text-base font-tibetan text-accent-secondary font-bold leading-[1.6]">གཞན་སྟོང་།</span>
              </div>
              <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-display font-bold text-ink leading-tight m-0">
                Shentong: "Empty of Other"
              </h3>
              <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono text-accent-secondary m-0">
                Great Madhyamaka (Mahā-Mādhyamaka) · Jonang Lineage (Maitreya & Dölpopa)
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Central Metaphor Highlight -->
            <div class="p-5 sm:p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-subtle border border-gold/40 flex flex-col gap-3 bg-gradient-to-br from-surface-subtle to-gold/10">
              <h4 class="font-display text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">
                The Golden Statue Hidden in Mud
              </h4>
              <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
                Buddha-nature is <strong>already complete with fully formed enlightened qualities</strong> (wisdom, bliss, compassion) beneath a thick layer of dried mud (defilements). The spiritual path is entirely subtractive: washing off adventitious stains until what was always present shines forth. The qualities of a Buddha are <em>revealed</em>, not produced.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Deep Doctrinal Explanation -->
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 text-sm sm:text-base text-ink/90 font-serif leading-relaxed">
              <p class="m-0">
                The Shentong position <strong>fully agrees</strong> with Rangtong that all conventional phenomena are empty of self-nature. But at the level of ultimate reality, when all adventitious defilements are cleared away, what remains is an <strong>unconditioned, primordial luminous awareness (<em>jñāna</em>)</strong> replete with inseparable Buddha-qualities.
              </p>
              <p class="m-0">
                This ultimate awareness is "empty of other" (<em>gzhan-stong</em>): devoid of temporary mental obscurations, yet <strong>not empty of its own enlightened nature</strong>. The Jonang tradition regards the Third Turning sūtras and Maitreya's <em>Ratnagotravibhāga</em> as the <strong>definitive, explicit teaching (<em>nītārtha</em>)</strong> of the Buddha.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>

          <!-- Bottom Affirmation Box -->
          <div class="border-t border-gold/30 flex flex-col gap-2 bg-gold/5 -mx-6 sm:-mx-8 lg:-mx-10 -mb-6 sm:-mb-8 lg:-mb-10 p-6 sm:p-8 lg:p-10 rounded-b-3xl relative z-10">
            <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent-secondary uppercase tracking-wider block">
              What Shentong Affirms:
            </span>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
              That ultimate reality is not a mere absence but a <em>luminous presence</em> — an unconditioned awareness possessing spontaneous enlightened qualities that have always existed innately within all sentient beings. Awakening does not create Buddhahood; it reveals what was never lost.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <!-- Full-Width Bottom Synthesis Strip -->
      <div class="surface-card p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl border border-border shadow-sm space-y-4 bg-surface">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-widest block">
            The Core Distinction in One Sentence:
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-sm sm:text-base font-serif">
          <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-border space-y-1.5">
            <span class="font-bold text-accent block font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm">Rangtong:</span>
            <p class="m-0 text-ink/90 leading-relaxed">
              <em>"Everything, including the ultimate, is empty of its own nature — there is nothing positive behind the negation."</em>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-xl bg-surface-subtle border border-gold/40 space-y-1.5 bg-gradient-to-br from-surface-subtle to-gold/5">
            <span class="font-bold text-accent-secondary block font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm">Shentong:</span>
            <p class="m-0 text-ink/90 leading-relaxed">
              <em>"Conventional things are empty of their own nature, but ultimate reality is a radiant awareness empty only of the temporary stains that cover it — and that awareness is replete with the qualities of Buddhahood."</em>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif pt-1">
          Both traditions resolutely reject any personal ego-self (<em>ātman</em>). The Shentong term "True Self" (<em>paramātman</em>) refers strictly to the complete transcendence of conceptual dualism — the indestructible, luminous nature of mind itself.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

