                  </div>
                  <h4 class="font-display text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">{y.name}</h4>
                  <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 pt-1 font-serif">{y.desc}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- VII. Chapter 6: Crisis, Suppression & Modern Resurgence -->
    <section id="suppression-resurgence" class="space-y-8 scroll-mt-[calc(var(--header-h)+4.5rem)] relative p-6 sm:p-10 rounded-3xl bg-gradient-to-br from-accent/8 via-surface to-surface-subtle border border-accent/25 shadow-xs overflow-hidden">
      <div class="relative z-10 space-y-8">
        <ChapterHeader
          chapter="Chapter 06"
          title="Spiritual Resilience and Living Resurgence"
          lede="The history of the Jonang lineage stands as an extraordinary testament to spiritual perseverance, preserving its sacred contemplative transmission through centuries of historical change to achieve unanimous contemporary recognition:"
        />

        <!-- Timeline Narrative Grid -->
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
          
          <div class="surface-card p-6 space-y-3 border-l-4 border-l-accent hover-lift bg-surface">
            <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase">1642 – 1658</span>
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">The 17th-Century Epoch</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
              During the complex political realignments of 17th-century Central Tibet, Jonang monastics demonstrated unwavering dedication to safeguarding the lineage. While outside observers mistakenly presumed the school had vanished from Tsang, Jonang masters ensured the complete philosophical treatises and esoteric Kālacakra transmissions were preserved intact.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="surface-card p-6 space-y-3 border-l-4 border-l-gold hover-lift bg-surface">
            <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase">18th – 19th Century</span>
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">Citadel of Amdo & Rimé Revival</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
              The tradition flourished with uninterrupted spiritual vitality in the mountain sanctuaries of Amdo, centered around the great <strong>Dzamthang Tsangwa Monastery</strong>. In the 19th century, luminaries of the non-sectarian <strong>Rimé movement</strong> (Jamgön Kongtrül, Jamyang Khyentse Wangpo) celebrated and championed Jonang Shentong scholarship.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="surface-card p-6 space-y-3 border-l-4 border-l-accent-secondary hover-lift bg-surface">
            <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase">2011 – Present</span>
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">Official Recognition & Flourishing</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed m-0 font-serif">
              In September 2011, at the 11th Religious Conference in Dharamshala, H.H. the 14th Dalai Lama and the leadership of all Tibetan spiritual traditions formally and unanimously recognized Jonang as the fifth major living school of Tibetan Buddhism. Today, the tradition flourishes worldwide across more than 60 monasteries in Tibet and the Himalayas, with Main Jonang Takten Phuntsok Choeling in Sanjauli, Shimla serving as the supreme seat, vibrant international spiritual center, and guardian of the living Kālacakra tradition in exile.
            </p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <!-- Archival Thangka Vignette: Dzamthang Amdo Heritage -->
        <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-accent/30 shadow-card grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 items-center relative overflow-hidden">
          <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono font-bold text-accent uppercase tracking-widest">Living Sanctuary · རྫམ་ཐང་དགོན་ཆེན།</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-display font-bold text-ink leading-snug m-0">
              The Mountain Citadel of Dzamthang & Archival Treasures
            </h3>
            <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-muted font-serif leading-relaxed m-0">
              In the isolated river valleys of Amdo Dzamthang, the Jonang monastic community maintained the uninterrupted transmission of the Kālacakra Six Yogas, carving thousands of new xylograph printing blocks and safeguarding priceless centuries-old thangkas and treatises. Today, Dzamthang stands as a living testament to the indestructible resilience of the lineage.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="lg:col-span-4 flex flex-col items-center justify-center">
            <div class="relative group rounded-xl overflow-hidden border-2 border-accent/40 shadow-md max-w-[200px]">
              <Image
                src={raudrantakaThangka}
                alt="Dzamthang protective thangka artwork"
                class="w-full h-auto object-cover transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:scale-105"
              />
              <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-black/70 via-transparent to-transparent flex items-end p-2">
                <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-tibetan text-white font-bold leading-tight">འཛམ་ཐང་དགོན་གྱི་གནའ་བོའི་ཐང་ག</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <span class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted font-mono mt-1.5 text-center">Dzamthang Archival Thangka Masterwork</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>

  <!-- FULL-WIDTH SECTION: CHAPTER 07 - COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS MATRIX -->
